expressions
===========

Compiling and evaluating algebraic expressions. Every expression in Pyxplot,
whether typed on its own, in a command, in a function definition or in a
"using" modifier, is compiled into bytecode by expCompile.c and executed by the
stack machine in expEval.c. Several modules split their header in two: a .h
file of types, and a _fns.h file of functions.

expCompile.c, expCompile.h, expCompile_fns.h
    The compiler. It tokenises an expression, checking which tokens may follow
    which to find syntax errors, and then compiles the tokens into bytecode
    with the shunting-yard algorithm. expCompile.h defines the bytecode
    instructions and the operator codes.

expEval.c, expEval.h
    The evaluator: a stack machine which executes the bytecode. It also casts
    values between types, and implements the string substitution operator %.

expEvalOps.c, expEvalOps.h
    The arithmetic operators, for every combination of types they accept:
    numbers with units, strings, dates, colors, vectors, matrices, lists and
    dictionaries.

expEvalSlice.c, expEvalSlice.h
    The [] operator, which takes items and slices from strings, lists,
    vectors, matrices and dictionaries.

expEvalCalculus.c, expEvalCalculus.h
    The int_dx() and diff_dx() functions, which numerically integrate and
    differentiate expressions using GSL.

fnCall.c, fnCall.h
    Calls function objects: built-in functions, user-defined functions,
    subroutines, and functions made by interpolate, histogram and fft. Also
    constructs objects when a type is called, e.g. vector(3).

dollarOp.c, dollarOp.h
    The $ operator, which refers to columns of the data file being read, e.g.
    in "plot 'data' using 1:$2**2".

traceback.c, traceback.h, traceback_fns.h
    Records errors with a traceback as they unwind through nested expressions,
    functions and commands, and prints them with arrows pointing to where
    each one occurred.

expThreadSafe.c, expThreadSafe.h
    Decides whether an expression can be evaluated on several threads at once,
    and creates the worker contexts in which threads evaluate expressions, e.g.
    when the plot command samples a function in parallel.
